  "text": "Questions two and three. What do you need to version to reproduce a model? And how do you version training data? Four things: the code, in git; the data, as a snapshot; the config, meaning hyperparameters, feature definitions and random seeds; and the trained artifact. A container image pins the libraries underneath. Data is the awkward one. Track snapshots beside each commit with a tool like DVC, data version control; query a warehouse with time travel, as of a past date; or write to immutable, date-stamped paths. The goal is the exact training set behind any past model. Why log the seed? Train a random forest on handwritten digits twenty times, changing nothing but the seed, and held-out accuracy runs from ninety-six point one to ninety-seven point eight percent. Fix the seed, and every prediction matches, run after run. So a one-point gain may just be a lucky seed: judge a change over several seeds, not one. And reproducible means the same code, data and config give back the same artifact.",
